{"input_text": "Question: I am trying to calculate the following integral  

 $$ \int_{0}^{\infty} \frac{\exp(-u^2)}{1+u^2} \, du. $$ 

 Wolfram gives a beautiful analytical answer: ${\rm e}\pi\operatorname{erfc}(1)$. I've tried every trick in my book (change of variable, contour, ...). I would love to see a proof of that beautiful result :)
Thanks in advance for any help. 

Answer: By the Schwinger trick: 
 \begin{equation}
\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{e}^{-u^2}}{1+u^2}\mathrm{d}u=\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{d}t\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathrm{e}^{-u^2}\mathrm{e}^{-t(1+u^2)}\mathrm{d}u= \int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{\sqrt{\pi } \mathrm{e}^{-t}}{2 \sqrt{t+1}} \mathrm{d}t=\frac{\mathrm{e\pi}}{2}\text{erfc}(1).
\end{equation} 
